# Slice 334 (Codex review of 333, item 2): the receiver fn binds its arguments by the one binder of a call against its
# contracts -- ordinary signatures every traversal registers when it begins (fn: name, args, return, var, body; name,
# args, return, body; name, args, return; name, parts) --: by position, by `:=`, and, for the parts of a receiver, by
# head. `fn: name := k1; args := ...` and `fn: name: k2; args: ...` (the author's two named forms, 2026-11-09) create
# their functions as the positional form does -- on entering the block, for their arguments are written -- with no
# callable named name here. The value of `part := (...)` is the body written after `:=`, read as the block of a part
# given by head is: several fields, none, a declaration, a statement. A part given by head is its Frame whatever it
# holds (none, one field, a vertical block). sub binds its own contracts by the same binder. Slice 335 (Codex's answer
# to the 334 questions: the author's `name := "test"` names test): a string literal gives a name by its value, its
# escapes decoded by the rules of C ("t\x33" names t3).
fn: name := k1; args := (int: a); return := int; body := (return: a + 1)
fn: name: k2; args: (int: a); return: int; body: (return: a * 3)
int: r1 k1(4)
int: r2 k2(4)
int: r3 k1(k2(1))
fn: name := k3; args := (int: a; int: b); return := int; var := (int: calls 0); body := (calls: calls + 1; return: a * 10 + b)
int: r4 k3(4; 2)
int: r5 k3(b := 1; a := 3)
int: c3 k3\var\calls
fn: name := k4; args := (); return := int; var := (int: n 7); body := (return: n)
fn: name: k5; args: (); return: int; var: (int: m 8); body: (return: m + 1)
int: r6 k4()
int: r7 k5()
fn:
    name: k6
    args:
        int: a
        int: b
    end: args
    return: int
    body:
        int: d a - b
        return: d * 2
    end: body
end: fn
int: r8 k6(9; 4)
sub: name := s1; args := (int: d 1); var := (int: total 0); body := (total: total + d)
s1(5)
s1()
int: total s1\var\total
fn: name := "t1"; args := (int: a); return := int; body := (return: a + 100)
fn: name: "t2"; args: (int: a); return: int; body: (return: a + 200)
fn: name := "t\x33"; args := (int: a); return := int; body := (return: a + 300)
int: r9 t1(1)
int: r10 t2(2)
int: r11 t3(3)
